From 437e22ff294f57569a1bfb68987a2fd40d38c2f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:59:36 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 29/40] lib/streamaggr: keep sample_timestamps sums per
 interval, not per timestamp

sample_timestamps kept a sum per distinct sample timestamp. vmagent scrapes
every target at an offset of its own, so an output fed by N targets kept N
sums and wrote N samples an interval, and N more for every interval it was
behind: on the lab cluster the option made vmagent fall further behind, and
it was killed at its 5 GiB limit. Samples are now kept under the end of the
interval they fall in, on the grid the flushes are aligned to: time.Truncate
counts from year 1, and for an interval that does not divide a day, such as
7m, the Unix epoch's grid is a different one. An output writes its pending
intervals as one series, and the slices holding more than one interval come
from a pool.

An adversarial review found two ways the option lost or moved data, both
already in the first version:

- A sum arriving after its interval was written went out at its timestamp
  less the flush's sequence number in milliseconds. Two timestamps a few
  milliseconds apart, written that many flushes apart, met on the same
  millisecond, and deduplication kept one of them. Interval ends are at
  least a second apart and the back-off stays under one.
- A flush running a few milliseconds after its boundary found samples of
  the interval still open, and backing off from the wall clock put them
  into the interval just closed. A flush now writes only the intervals
  that ended by its boundary, each at its end and in order, and leaves
  the later ones to the flushes at their ends; every write stays within
  its interval and not after the wall clock, which covers the flush on
  shutdown. Leaving only the next interval, as this first did, wrote the
  one after it early when a flush took longer than its interval, and
  sum_samples_total, never writing a timestamp before one it has written,
  then moved the earlier interval into the later one. An entry holding
  sums no flush has written yet is not dropped as stale until they are.

The heartbeat of sum_samples_total goes no further than the flush's
boundary, and vm_streamaggr_output_samples_total counts samples rather than
series. Without the option the outputs do not change.

With 20 targets and 2000 outputs on 1m, per flush: 20 -> 2 samples an
output, 11.8 -> 7.8 ms (7.5 ms without the option), 14264 -> 547
allocations; four minutes behind, 60 -> 3.75 samples and 45.0 -> 30.5 ms
(29.5 ms). A randomized test checks every interval against the sums of its
samples' timestamps, with targets at offsets of their own, rounds up to
150 seconds late, flushes running up to 100 seconds over and a flush on
shutdown; it fails on the first version in every seed. Holding the open
interval back, keeping each write within its interval, keeping a stale
entry with pending sums, and a flush longer than its interval each have a
test that fails without the change. The output is
correct with -dedup.minScrapeInterval=1ms and one vmagent writing each
series, as ACG runs it. The option stays off until a full-scale replay
confirms it.
